Electrical discharge machining of Hastelloy alloys is sensitive to the physical and microstructural properties of the electrode material, which influence process efficiency, machining accuracy, and surface roughness. Nowicki et al. report that graphite is increasingly used for EDM electrodes because of low raw material cost, good machinability, and high sublimation temperature, and that grain size markedly affects material removal rate (MRR), tool wear rate (TWR), and surface roughness (Ra) in EDM of Hastelloy C-22. {{cite:S1#c1}} Their experiments with POCO graphite grades (1 µm AF-5 and 10 µm S-180) show larger-grain graphite gives higher process efficiency but higher relative tool wear, whereas finer-grain graphite yields the lowest Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}}

A later study by the same group frames the grain-size effect as a challenge for industrial implementation but reports that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear in EDM of Hastelloy C-22; detached graphite grains are observed to disturb discharge stability and process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}} Both studies derive empirical or mathematical models linking machining parameters and electrode grain size to technological indicators and surface texture, enabling prediction of machining outcomes under the tested conditions. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}

Across EDM studies of high-performance alloys, discharge energy parameters—peak current and pulse duration—consistently emerge as dominant drivers of MRR, TWR, and surface finish. Nowicki et al. identify discharge current and pulse duration as the main factors controlling MRR and Ra for both graphite grades, and time interval as dominant for relative electrode wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} Ramabalan et al. likewise find current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode wear, taper, and profile changes (e.g., transformation of sharp edges toward hemispherical ends) in EDM of Hastelloy B2. {{cite:S4#c1}} Experimental design and ANOVA analyses in other alloy studies corroborate the primacy of peak current (amperage) for MRR, TWR, and Ra outcomes. {{cite:S2#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

A range of adjunct process modifications aim to mitigate high TWR and low MRR inherent to EDM. Reviews and experimental reports summarize strategies including cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance, and electrical-discharge coating to improve MRR, reduce TWR, or modify surface integrity; these works discuss effects on MRR, TWR, Ra, and recast layers and identify gaps for further research. {{cite:S6#c1}}{{cite:S5#c1}} For example, Taguchi-based optimization found cryogenic treatment type, pulse-on time, and peak current jointly determine optimal Ra and MRR in a corrosion-resistant superalloy, with peak current explaining the largest share of variance. {{cite:S2#c1}} Studies on nanopowder-mixed EDM and electrode cryogenic treatment report substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut for brass electrodes under specific dielectric conditions, illustrating that electrode treatment and dielectric additives can strongly influence electrode life and dimensional accuracy. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Despite these findings for graphite and for process variants, direct comparisons between composite electrodes and conventional electrodes (graphite, copper, brass) specifically in EDM of Hastelloy alloys are not represented in the supplied literature. [[NEEDS SOURCE: Comparative studies of composite electrodes on Hastelloy]]

rows.bRaw:
Graphite electrode grain size strongly controls EDM outcomes on Hastelloy C-22, with finer grains reducing tool wear and coarser grains increasing process efficiency at the cost of higher relative tool wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Experimental comparisons using POCO AF-5 (1 µm) and POCO S-180 (10 µm) show that coarser graphite yields higher material removal rate while finer graphite produces lower surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

A complementary study that also tested AF-5 and EDM-180 reported that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear, and that detached graphite grains influence discharge stability and removal mechanisms. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

Both studies modelled the dependence of machining indicators on electrical parameters and concluded that discharge current and pulse duration are the principal controls of MRR and Ra for the graphite grades tested. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} 

Relative tool wear of graphite electrodes is reported to be dominated by the time interval between discharges in the experiments on Hastelloy. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Across nickel-based and other difficult-to-machine alloys, peak current (amperage) appears repeatedly as the most influential parameter on MRR and tool wear, with ANOVA results attributing the largest share of variability to current in Taguchi-designed experiments. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} 

Process instability at sharp electrode features promotes shape degradation and increased wear: square electrodes developed hemispherical ends when sparks became unstable, indicating that current and pulse duration govern wear-driven geometry change. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

Broader reviews of EDM interventions identify multiple approaches—cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance and others—that modify MRR, TWR and surface integrity, and which represent potential routes to mitigate high tool wear and roughness. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Nanopowder in the dielectric and cryogenic treatment of electrodes have been reported to reduce electrode wear and improve dimensional accuracy in other alloys, suggesting similar strategies could be explored for Hastelloy EDM. {{cite:S7#c1}} 

Electrical discharge coating (EDC) is noted as an adjacent technology for producing modified surface layers via discharges, but its direct relevance to electrode selection in EDM of Hastelloy is not established in the cited literature. {{cite:S5#c1}} 

[[NEEDS SOURCE: Comparative data for composite vs conventional electrodes on Hastelloy]]

