Selective laser melting (SLM) produces near-fully dense maraging-steel components by layer-wise melting and rapid solidification, a process that promotes unique microstructural features compared with conventional routes. {{cite:S1#c1}} The as-built SLM microstructure is reported as a fine martensitic matrix with submicron grain sizes and abundant needle-shaped nanoprecipitates Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) in some studies, which is attributed to extremely high cooling rates during fabrication. {{cite:S3#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. observed a fine-grain martensitic structure in as-printed maraging steel with no preferred texture or anisotropy across build sections and a surface roughness Ra ≈ 10 µm. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Heat treatments applied to SLM maraging steels modify these as-built features by promoting precipitation, relieving residual stress, and—in some regimes—reverting martensite to austenite. Tan et al. applied heat treatments chosen from phase-transformation temperatures and found that aging produces massive needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates responsible for age hardening, while the treatments significantly relieve residual stress and slightly reduce mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Godec et al. investigated the interaction of prior solution treatment and aging with subsequent plasma nitriding and reported that thermochemical treatment following prior heat treatment improves wear and corrosion resistance relative to nitriding without prior aging, where cracks in the compound layer occurred. {{cite:S2#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. compared SLM and wrought maraging steels in as-printed, austenitized/quenched and aged conditions, and concluded that despite the finer SLM microstructure, nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour in heat-treated conditions were similar to conventional wrought material, with precipitation playing a primary role in both hardness and corrosion potential. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Several studies address austenite reversion (reversed austenite) and its influence on corrosion. Khan et al. tempered SLM maraging steel in the intercritical α' + γ range near 720°C to induce reversed austenite, finding that reversed austenite nucleates mainly at martensite lath boundaries where local Ni enrichment occurs due to intermetallic dissolution. {{cite:S4#c1}} Electrochemical testing in 3.5 wt% NaCl showed the 720°C-tempered specimen—containing reversed austenite—exhibited higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, and homogenized specimens without reversed austenite; the authors conclude reversed austenite enhances pitting resistance in chloride solution. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Reviews of AM steels synthesize these findings and highlight both advantages and remaining challenges: AM induces hierarchical (sub)grains and fine precipitates that can enhance strength, wear and corrosion resistance compared with conventional steels, while achieving acceptable ductility and fatigue remains problematic and in situ phase transformations during AM act as intrinsic heat treatments that influence final properties. {{cite:S6#c1}} Afkhami et al. similarly note that heat treatment, build orientation and surface quality are influential parameters for performance of SLM steels, indicating gaps remain in comprehensive understanding of property interrelations. {{cite:S5#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparisons of chloride pitting rates across treatments]]

rows.bRaw:
Selective laser melting (SLM) of 300‑grade maraging steel produces an ultra-fine martensitic matrix with submicron grains and dense populations of nanoscale Ni3X precipitates that arise from very high cooling rates. {{cite:S3#c1}}

The SLM process builds parts layer by layer by selectively melting metallic powder with a high power‑density laser, and it is reported to produce near–full‑density components reaching up to 99.9% relative density. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Because SLM imposes repeated rapid thermal cycles, it can act as an intrinsic heat treatment that triggers in situ tempering, precipitation and other phase transformations in precipitation‑hardening steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Microstructural characterization across multiple build planes of SLM maraging steel finds a fine martensitic structure with little evidence of a preferred crystallographic texture or anisotropy in grain morphology. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Measured average subgrain sizes of around $0.31\ \mu$m indicate cooling rates on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$ in optimised SLM builds. {{cite:S3#c1}}

In the as‑built condition SLM maraging steels contain dense, needle‑shaped nanoprecipitates (Ni3Ti, Ni3Al, Ni3Mo) within the martensitic matrix, and these precipitates account for subsequent age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Heat treatments applied according to detected phase transformation temperatures modify residual stress, precipitate state and mechanical anisotropy: aging increases hardness while reducing residual stresses and slightly reducing orientation‑dependent mechanical differences. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Comparisons between heat‑treated SLM and conventionally produced maraging steels show that, despite the finer as‑built microstructure, nano‑hardness and corrosion behaviour after appropriate heat treatment can be similar between SLM and wrought material. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Aging produces the maximum nano‑hardness and corresponds to the lowest measured corrosion potential in the studies that compared as‑printed, austenitised/quenched and aged conditions, indicating precipitation strongly influences both hardness and electrochemical response. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Targeted intercritical tempering that induces martensite‑to‑austenite reversion deposits reversed austenite primarily along martensite lath boundaries, with local nickel enrichment from dissolved intermetallics promoting reversion nucleation. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Specimens tempered at an intercritical condition near $720\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ that developed reversed austenite show higher pitting potentials and lower corrosion current densities in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as‑printed, aged, or homogenized specimens lacking reverted austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Thermochemical nitriding performed at $520\ ^\circ\mathrm{C}$ for 6 h can be combined with prior solution treatment and aging, but nitriding without prior aging produced cracks in the compound nitride layer of SLM maraging steel. {{cite:S2#c1}}

When nitriding follows prior heat treatment, the thermochemical treatment provides wear and corrosion resistance benefits relative to untreated SLM material, though origins of nitride layer cracks and pores are identified and discussed. {{cite:S2#c1}}

SLM maraging steel composites containing WC reinforcements achieve higher densification and lower porosity than some alternative AM routes, and this densification correlates with improved wear performance in comparative studies. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Despite many reported performance enhancements obtained through process control and post‑processing, gaps remain in understanding fatigue and other service‑condition behaviours of SLM steels due to the large parameter space of the process. {{cite:S5#c1}}

