Household-level studies identify four recurrent frictions between awareness and installation: informational gaps, procedural complexity, structural limitations, and perceived financial risk. {{cite:S1#c2}}

Bagla’s qualitative study in Karnataka used semi-structured interviews and questionnaires with 70 households to probe the “last mile” frictions that prevent subsidy uptake, and triangulated findings with national survey evidence. {{cite:S1#c1}} Bagla reports that non-adopters rank space and roof-ownership constraints as the leading barrier, followed by information gaps and upfront cost, while adopters rate environmental concern as their primary motivation. {{cite:S1#c1}} The study quantifies a notable divergence between anticipated and experienced procedural burden: adopters expected installation complexity at a mean of $5.1$ out of $10$ before installation but reported actual complexity of $2.2$ after installation. {{cite:S1#c1}} Bagla also documents a “subsidy paradox” in which restrictions on panels, vendor empanelment, and paperwork dilute the practical value of capital subsidies, and notes that $45.8\%$ of adopters stated they would have adopted even without the subsidy. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Studies from other Indian states and international case studies corroborate and nuance these themes. N.M & A.H apply the Theory of Planned Behaviour in North Kerala using a structured questionnaire and structural equation modelling, and find that environmental concern, government subsidy awareness, and cost–benefit perception positively affect attitudes toward rooftop solar; attitude, subjective norms, and perceived behavioural control then significantly influence installation intention, with the structural model explaining $30.3\%$ of attitude variance and $26.8\%$ of intention variance. {{cite:S6#c1}} Panwar & Yadav’s survey in Meerut similarly highlights cost, environmental consciousness, government incentives and technological reliability as salient determinants of household perception and reports that adoption remains below desired levels. {{cite:S5#c1}}

Quantitative and theory-driven work links perceived product attributes to purchase intention. Kumar & Kaushik use diffusion of innovation dimensions with data from $863$ respondents and structural equation modelling to show that relative advantage, compatibility, complexity, innovativeness and observability positively influence purchase intention, while trialability does not. {{cite:S4#c1}} Kumar’s Patna study employed a $200$-respondent questionnaire and factor analysis to identify context-specific barriers that require collaboration among government, providers and the public. {{cite:S3#c1}} Outside India, Chidembo et al.’s village case studies used purposive sampling and thematic network analysis to find that rural households often perceive solar PV as cheap and environmentally friendly but simultaneously express doubts about energy quality, theft risk, and high capital requirements, motivating calls for awareness programmes and inclusive financing. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Across studies, two patterns emerge: (1) subjective perceptions of complexity, cost and reliability shape intention strongly, and (2) procedural and structural constraints—roof ownership, vendor rules and paperwork—can erode the effective value of subsidies. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}} [[NEEDS SOURCE: Comparative evidence on subsidy administrative costs]]

rows.bRaw:
Household adoption of rooftop solar in Karnataka is constrained by interacting informational, procedural, structural and financial frictions that persist despite a progressive policy framework. {{cite:S1#c2}}

A qualitative study of 70 Karnataka households—24 adopters and 46 non-adopters—found that non-adopters most frequently cited roof space and roof-ownership limits as their primary barrier, followed by information shortfalls and upfront cost concerns. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

That same study measured households’ expectations and experiences of procedural complexity: prospective adopters rated expected complexity at a mean of $5.1$ out of $10$ before installation and actual experienced complexity at $2.2$ after installation, a $57$ per cent reduction. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Adopters nevertheless reported mixed effects of subsidies: $45.8$ per cent said they would have installed even without the subsidy, while others described a “subsidy paradox” in which restrictions on panel types, vendor empanelment and paperwork diluted the subsidy’s practical value. {{cite:S1#c1}} 

Household perceptions documented in small rural case studies complement these findings: many households view solar PV as relatively cheap, easy to use and environmentally friendly, yet some perceive the technology as producing inferior quality power, vulnerable to theft, and requiring prohibitive capital outlay. {{cite:S2#c1}} 

Quantitative analyses across larger Indian samples show that perceived complexity, relative advantage, compatibility, observability and innovativeness of rooftop systems are statistically linked to purchase intentions, with trialability not predicting intention in one national study. {{cite:S4#c1}} 

Behavioural determinants also matter: a structural equation model using the Theory of Planned Behaviour in North Kerala found that environmental concern, awareness of government subsidies and cost–benefit perceptions positively affected attitudes, and that attitude, subjective norms and perceived behavioural control significantly predicted installation intention. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

That model explained $30.3\%$ of the variance in attitude and $26.8\%$ of the variance in intention in the surveyed sample. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Context-specific surveys indicate that barriers and levers differ by place: urban residents’ responses in Patna emphasise institutional coordination and public participation as prerequisites for uptake, implying that solutions require collaboration across government, utilities and the public. {{cite:S3#c1}} 

Several studies therefore converge on a pattern: supply-side procedural simplification and clearer information reduce perceived complexity, while structural constraints (roof area, ownership) and liquidity constraints keep many households from translating willingness into installation. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}} 

Policy interventions should address the bundle of frictions—targeted outreach to correct quality and reliability perceptions, streamlined paperwork and broader vendor empanelment, mechanisms to relax roof-ownership and financing constraints—to align subsidy design with household realities. {{cite:S1#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}
